cleId></ArticleIdList><ReferenceList><Title/><Reference><Citation>Alexander M (1994) Cometabolism. Biodegradation and bioremediation. Academic Press, New York</Citation></Reference><Reference><Citation>Anderson JE, McCarty PL (1997) Transformation yields of chlorinated ethenes by a methanotrophic mixed culture expressing particulate methane monooxygenase. Appl Environ Microbiol 63:687&#x2013;693</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">9023946</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">168358</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1128/aem.63.2.687-693.1997</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Auman AJ, Stolyar S, Costello AM, Lidstrom ME (2000) Molecular characterization of methanotrophic isolates from freshwater lake sediment. Appl Environ Microbiol 66:5259&#x2013;5266</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">11097900</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">92454</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1128/AEM.66.12.5259-5266.2000</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Beck DA, Kalyuzhnaya MG, Malfatti S, Tringe SG, Glavina Del Rio T, Ivanova N, Lidstrom ME, Chistoserdova L (2013) A metagenomic insight into freshwater methane-utilizing communities and evidence for cooperation between the Methylococcaceae and the Methylophilaceae. PeerJ 1:e23</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">23638358</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">3628875</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.7717/peerj.23</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Belova SE, Kulichevskaya IS, Bodelier PL, Dedysh SN (2013) Methylocystis bryophila sp. nov., a facultatively methanotrophic bacterium from acidic Sphagnum peat, and emended description of the genus Methylocystis (ex Whittenbury et al. 1970) Bowman et al. 1993. Int J Syst Evol 63:1096&#x2013;1104</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1099/ijs.0.043505-0</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Bolyen E et al (2019) Reproducible, inte